" focusable="false" focused="false" scrollable="false" long-clickable="false" password="false" selected="false" bounds="[990,2109][1001,2130]" /></node></node></node></node></node><node index="5" text="" resource-id="" class="android.view.ViewGroup" package="com.kalsa.app" content-desc="" checkable="false" checked="false" clickable="false" enabled="true" focusable="false" focused="false" scrollable="false" long-clickable="false" password="false" selected="false" bounds="[0,803][1080,1514]" /><node index="6" text="" resource-id="" class="android.view.ViewGroup" package="com.kalsa.app" content-desc="" checkable="false" checked="false" clickable="false" enabled="true" focusable="false" focused="false" scrollable="false" long-clickable="false" password="false" selected="false" bounds="[42,836][1038,1364]" /><node index="7" text="" resource-id="" class="android.view.ViewGroup" package="com.kalsa.app" content-desc="" checkable="false" checked="false" clickable="true" enabled="true" focusable="true" focused="false" scrollable="false" long-clickable="false" password="false" selected="false" bounds="[63,857][1017,1217]"><node index="0" text="write a careful field note about a long mountain journey and explain how a patient observer should separate signal from noise the journey begins before sunrise when the valley is quiet and the trail is cool a small team checks water maps food clothing and instruments before leaving the shelter each person carries a different task but everyone shares the same route the first marker is a river crossing where the current is clear and the stones are stable the team records the time the air temperature the cloud cover and the condition of the path  the trail climbs through cedar forest where the light changes slowly between branches the observer notices that distance is not the same as effort because a steep short slope can demand more work than a long gentle traverse the team walks at a steady pace and pauses at fixed landmarks each pause is long enough to breathe but short enough to preserve rhythm the notes describe what happened rather than what was expected the observer avoids dramatic language and keeps the order of events clear so another reader can reconstruct the day  above the forest the ground opens into a wide basin with grass low shrubs and scattered stone the wind crosses the basin from the west and carries the smell of rain the team compares the current reading with the earlier reading and writes both values without replacing one with the other a change may be real or it may come from a different angle a warm hand a loose strap or a shaded instrument repeated observations help reveal which explanation is likely the team keeps every observation even when it seems inconvenient  the central ridge is visible beyond the basin and the route follows a narrow shelf toward it the shelf is safe when the group stays close to the inside wall but loose gravel makes haste unwise the leader sets a pace that allows every member to remain in contact the observer records the pace the pauses and the reason for each pause a useful record includes failures as well as successes because a difficult section may explain a later delay a missing value is never silently treated as an ordinary value  at midday the team reaches a flat shoulder beneath the ridge the sun is bright and the rock holds heat the group rests in a place with a clear view of the trail ahead water is measured before and after the rest and the instruments are placed in shade the observer asks whether the reading describes the environment or the instrument itself this question prevents a local artifact from becoming a broad conclusion the team also checks whether the route has changed since the morning and marks any new obstruction  the ascent from the shoulder is slower because the air is thin and the stones are uneven each step is chosen for balance rather than speed the team follows a simple rule when a member feels uncertain the group stops and confirms the next safe position the observer records the rule and the moment when it was used a rule that prevents a stall is different from a rule that merely delays action the distinction matters when a system must adapt to changing conditions while still completing its task  near the ridge the weather turns and a thin cloud covers the summit the team does not infer a storm from one cold gust instead it watches the sky the wind and the trail together a decision is made only after several observations agree the group continues when the route is open and turns toward a lower saddle when visibility falls the observer writes the decision the evidence and the alternative that was rejected this makes the account useful even if the final outcome is not the one first desired  the lower saddle leads to a sheltered ledge where the team can wait without blocking the trail the ledge is warm on one side and cool on the other so measurements depend on where an instrument is placed the observer labels each position and never mixes readings from different positions a later comparison uses matching positions and matching intervals the team checks batteries cables and memory before collecting another sample because a change in equipment can look like a change in the mountain  in the afternoon the cloud breaks and the ridge becomes visible again the team continues to a small overlook above the valley from there the river appears as a narrow silver line and the morning shelter is hidden by trees the observer reviews the notes and look" resource-id="" class="android.widget.EditText" package="com.kalsa.app" content-desc="" checkable="false" checked="false" clickable="true" enabled="true" focusable="true" focused="true" scrollable="false" long-clickable="true" password="false" selected="false" bounds="[63,857][1017,1217]" /></node><node index="8" text="" resource-id="" class="android.widget.Button" package="com.kalsa.app" content-desc="Add attachment" checkable="false" checked="false